Subject: JSON.parse takes the expected-key path for literals and nested values too

What this does
  - the direct parser's expected-key path (0595, 0597) also reads true, false and null in place, and parses an object or array value recursively before storing it with the cached next shape
  - `JSONShapeCache.gen`: bumped whenever json_obj_add drops chain entries; after a nested value the entry is used only if the generation is unchanged and the object still has the chain's k-th shape, else the key is interned again from its input bytes (JS_NewAtomLen) and added by json_obj_add as on the general path

Why we need it
  Booleans, nulls, nested objects and arrays (4 of 11 members of an API record here) still went through the key token, an atom reference and json_obj_add. json_app parse only: -8.3% cycles over two runs, -8.1% instructions.

Soundness
  The literal checks are those of json_fast_value. A nested value can change this chain only by dropping entries (a slot collision diverging it), which bumps gen; extensions add entries at or past n and leave entry k and its atom as they were, and the chain's shapes hold its atoms. On a change the key bytes, plain ASCII, give the same atom the key reader would, and json_obj_add then does what it did before this patch. Differentials identical old vs new: new jgen.js (28,000 checks, 4,000 records whose nested objects share the parent's first key and collide with its slot, with the fallback taken 1,125 times, counted with a debugger breakpoint; also under QJS_JSON_LAZY=1), jrec, jslot, jk16, jdiff, jdiff2, jedge, jscan, jnum, jld.

